          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            What is known as the
            %strong Tychonoff theorem
            or as
            %strong Tychonoff’s theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#Tychonoff35"}> Tychonoff 35
            ) is a basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
            in the field of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            (with its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+spaces"} compact topological spaces
            is itself
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mtext compact
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mtext compact
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\underset{i \in I}{\forall}
                  \\left(
                  X_i \,\text{compact}
                  \\right)
                  \\right)
                  \\;\Rightarrow\;
                  \\left(
                  \\underset{i \in I}{\prod} X_i
                  \\,\text{compact}
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
            of factors the proof of this statement is elementary (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#ElementaryProofOfFinitaryCase"} below
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of factors the proof is non-trivial, but is conveniently given using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ProofViaNets"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilters"} ultrafilters
            (
            %a{:href => "#ProofViaUltrafilters"}> further below
            ).
          %p
            In its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical
            form, Tychonoff’s theorem is equivalent to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            if each set is inhabited.)
          %p
            Notice that no
            %a{:href => "axiom+of+choice#Variants"} choice
            whatsoever is needed to prove the analogous theorem for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of locales is compact if each of the locales is compact. From that perspective, the statement equivalent to the axiom of choice is this: a product of locales is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+locale"} spatial
            if each of the locales is spatial. Either of these may be considered a Tychonoff theorem for locales.
          %p
            To prove Tychonoff’s theorem for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+spaces"} Hausdorff topological spaces
            only, the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            is not needed; the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
            (and possibly
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %h2#proofs Proofs
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ElementaryProofOfFinitaryCase"} elementary proof for the finitary case
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ElementaryProofViaClosedProjectionCharacterization"} elementary proof via closed-projection characterization
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ProofViaNets"} proof via net convergence
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ProofViaUltrafilters"} proof via ultrafilter convergence
          %h3#ElementaryProofOfFinitaryCase Elementary proof of finitary case
          %p
            The finitary case of Tychonoff’s theorem, where products with only a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
            of factors are considered, may be proven with elementary means from basic topology. Such a proof is spelled out
            %em
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Topology+--+1#BinaryTychonoffTheorem"} here
            at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Topology+--+1"}> Introduction to Topology – 1
              \:
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p of the finitary Tychonoff theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
              be two twopological spaces, with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ U_i \subset X \times Y \}_{i \in I}
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of the product space. We need to show that this has a finite subcover.
            %p
              By definition of the product space topology, each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              is the union, indexed by some set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+products"} Cartesian products
              of open subsets of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi k
                            %mi i
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi k
                            %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mphantom
                      %mi A
                    %mtext and
                    %mphantom
                      %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    U_i
                    \\;=\;
                    \\underset{k_i \in K_i}{\cup} \left(
                    V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}
                    \\right)
                    \\phantom{AAAA}
                    V_{k_i} \in \tau_{X} \,\phantom{A}\text{and} \phantom{A}\, W_{k_i} \in \tau_{Y}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Consider then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
              of all these index sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    K
                    \\,\coloneqq\,
                    \\underset{i \in I}{\sqcup}
                    K_i
                    \\,.
            %p This is such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AA
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi k
                              %mi i
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi k
                              %mi i
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                        %mo }
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\star)
                    \\phantom{AA}
                    \\left\{
                    V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}
                    \\subset
                    X \times Y
                    \\right\}_{k_i \in K}
            %p
              is again an open cover of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
            %p
              But by construction, each element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}
              of this new cover is contained in at least one
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{j(k_i)}
              of the original cover. Therefore it is now sufficient to show that there is a finite subcover of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\star)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi fin
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i \in K_{fin} \subset K
              for some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi fin
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{fin}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{j(k_i)}
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi fin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i \in K_{fin}
              form a finite subcover of the original cover.
            %p
              In order to see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\star)
              has a finite subcover, first fix a point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              and write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\} \subset X
              for the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+subspace"} topological subspace
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
              to the abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{x\} \times Y \simeq Y
                    \\,.
            %p
              Therefore, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\tau_Y)
              is assumed to be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi k
                                %mi i
                          %mo ×
                          %msub
                            %mi W
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi k
                                %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                        %mo }
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left\{
                    \\left(
                    (V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}) \cap (\{x\} \times Y)
                    \\right)
                    \\;\subset\;
                    \\{x\}\times Y
                    \\right\}_{k_i \in K}
            %p
              has a finite subcover, indexed by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi J
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_x \subset K
            %p
              Here we may assume without restriction of generality that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in V_{k_i}
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi J
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i \in J_x \subset K
            %p
              By finiteness of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_x
              it now follows that the intersection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ∩
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V_x
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\underset{k_i \in J_x}{\cap} V_{k_i}
            %p is still an open subset, and by the previous remark we may assume without restriction that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x \in V_x
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now observe that by the nature of the above cover of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\} \times Y
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊂
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi x
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{x\} \times Y
                    \\subset
                    \\underset{k_i \in J_x \subset K}{\cup}  V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}
            %p and hence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi x
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{x\} \times Y \subset \{x\} \times \underset{k_i \in J_x \subset K}{\cup} W_{k_i}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since by construction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x \subset V_{k_i}
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi J
                        %mi x
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i \in J_x \subset K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\} \times Y
              but of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x \times Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ⊂
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi x
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi k
                            %mi i
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi k
                            %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V_x \times Y
                    \\subset
                    \\underset{k_i \in J_x \subset K}{\cup} \left( V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i} \right)
                    \\,.
            %p
              To conclude, observe that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_x \subset X\}_{x \in X}
              is clearly an open cover of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is compact there is a finite set of points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subset X
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi x
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_x \subset X\}_{x \in S \subset X}
              is still a cover. In summary then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo {
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi k
                              %mi i
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi k
                              %mi i
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                        %mo }
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⊂
                            %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi k
                              %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %msub
                              %mi J
                              %mi x
                            %mo ⊂
                            %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left\{
                    V_{k_i} \times W_{k_i}
                    \\subset X \times Y
                    \\right\}_{{x \in S \subset X} \atop { k_i \in J_x \subset K }}
            %p is a finite subcover as required. t
          %h3#ElementaryProofViaClosedProjectionCharacterization Elementary proof of general case via closed-projection characterization
          %p
            Another equivalent characterization of compactness of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is that for all spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_Y \;\colon\; Y \times X \to Y
            out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+map"} closed map
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed-projection+characterization+of+compactness"} closed-projection characterization of compactness
          %p
            This characterization allows an elementary proof of the general Tychonoff theorem, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "closed-projection+characterization+of+compactness#TychonoffTheorem"} there
          %h3#ProofViaNets Proof via net convergence
          %p
            We give the proof of the Tychonoff theorem using the characterization of compactness via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
            (
            %a{:href => "net#CompactSpacesEquivalentlyHaveConvergetSubnets"}> ths prop
            ). This proof is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Chernoff92"}> Chernoff 92
            ).
          #ProofOfTychonoffTheoremViaNets.proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              (of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem"} Tychonoff theorem
              via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergence
              of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nets"} nets
            %p
              By
              %a{:href => "net#CompactSpacesEquivalentlyHaveConvergetSubnets"} this prop.
              a topological space is compact precisely if every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
              in that space has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergent
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subnet"} subnet
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cluster+point"} cluster point
            %p So let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nu \;\colon\; A \to \underset{i \in I}{\prod} X_i
            %p be a net in the product space. We need to show that this has a cluster point.
            %p
              For every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \subset I
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi I
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi ν
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi pr
                          %mi J
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nu\vert_I
                    \\;\colon\;
                    A
                    \\overset{\nu}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\underset{i \in I}{\prod} X_i
                    \\overset{pr_J}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\underset{i \in J \subset I}{\prod}
                    X_i
            %p
              for the image of the net under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              to the product space of factors with indices in the subset
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %p
              Say that a
              %em partial cluster point
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a finite subset
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi J
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \subset I
              %li
                %p
                  a cluster point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mi J
                        %mo ∈
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi J
                            %mo ⊂
                            %mi I
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_J \in \underset{i \in J \subset I}{\prod} X_i
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ν
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi J
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu\vert_J
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PartialClusterPt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PartialClusterPt(\nu)
              for the set of all partial cluster points of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
              given by the evident extension of domains.
            %p
              Observe that, by definition, a partial cluster point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_J
              for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo =
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J = I
              is an actual cluster point of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            %p
              We claim now that if the partially ordered set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi PartialClusterPt
                      %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (PartialClusterPt_{\leq})
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+element"} maximal element
              then this is an actual cluster point of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            %p
              To see this, assume it were not, hence that the maximal element were a partial cluster point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_J
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo \
                      %mi J
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \backslash J \neq \emptyset
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                      %mo \
                      %mi J
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in I\backslash J
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_k
              is a compact space by assumption, it would follow that the net
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu\vert_{\{k\}}
              had a cluster point
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_J
              would yield a partial cluster point with larger domain
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \cup \{k\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_J
              is already maximal. Hence we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+by+contradiction"} proof by contradiction
              that every maximal element in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PartialClusterPt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PartialClusterPt(\nu)_{\leq}
              is an actual cluster point.
            %p
              Therefore we may now conclude by showing that the partially ordered set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PartialClusterPt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PartialClusterPt(\nu)_{\leq}
              does have a maximal element. To this end we invoke
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PartialClusterPt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PartialClusterPt(\nu)
              is not
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mo ≤
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi PartialClusterPt
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\leq} \subset PartialClusterPt(\nu)_{\leq}
              on which the partial order restricts to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
              has an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+bound"} upper bound
              partial cluster point.
            %p
              Now non-emptyness follows immediately because there is always the partial cluster point with empty domain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{\emptyset}
            %p
              Hence consider a totally ordered subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \{ (c_k)_{J_k} \}_{k \in K}
              of partial cluster points.
            %p For
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi tot
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    J_{tot} \coloneqq \underset{k \in K}{\cup} J_k
            %p the union of the domains of the partial cluster points in the subset, then this induces a point
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi tot
                    %mo ∈
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi tot
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    c_{tot} \in \underset{ i \in J_{tot}  }{\prod} X_i
                    \\,.
            %p
              If this is a partial cluster point, then it is clearly an upper bound of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              is indeed a partial cluster point.
            %p
              This means that we need to show that for every Tychonoff-basic open neighbourhood
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi tot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \underset{i \in J_{tot}}{\prod}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi tot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{tot}
              and every element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
              (in the domain of the net) there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≥
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \geq a
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ν
                        %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_b \in U
            %p
              But by definition of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi tot
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{i \in J_{tot}}{\prod} U_i
              of open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \subset X_i
              such that only over a finite subset of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi tot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_{tot}
              they differ from the total spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi tot
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{tot}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi tot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_{tot}
              must be contained in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_k
              for some
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi J
                          %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{J_k}
              is a partial cluster point, the claim follows.
          %h3#ProofViaUltrafilters Proof via ultrafilter convergence
          %p
            One method of proof uses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergence
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a{:href => "#BourbakiGeneralTopology"} Bourbaki’s proof
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Cartan37"} Cartan 37
          %h4#first_version First version
          #UltrafilterProof.proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle X_\alpha \rangle_{\alpha \in A}
              be a family of compact spaces.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Every ultrafilter
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  on the underlying set of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\alpha
                  converges to some point
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+intersection+property"} finite intersection property
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a{:href => "compact+space#fip"} this prop.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
                  be a point belonging to that intersection. Every closed set disjoint from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
                  belongs to the complement of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
                  belongs to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  converges to
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
              %li
                %p
                  Let
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  be an ultrafilter on
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                            %mi α
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_\alpha X_\alpha
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi β
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \mapsto \beta S
                  is
                  = succeed "ial," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %mi β
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha = \beta(\pi_\alpha)(U)
                  on each
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mi α
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\alpha
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
