                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_\alpha
                  is a projection map. Choose a point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
                  to which
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\alpha
                  converges. Then it is easily checked that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  converges to the point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x_\alpha \rangle
                  in the product space.
              %li
                %p
                  Since every ultrafilter
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mi α
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_\alpha X_\alpha
                  converges to a point, the product is compact. (If it were not, then we could find a collection of nonempty closed sets whose intersection is empty, but closed under finite intersections. This collection generates a filter which is contained in some ultrafilter
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                  converges to some
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          #TyCH.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
              is used in step 2 of the proof
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#UltrafilterProof"} abov
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
              into a single family
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x_\alpha \rangle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\alpha
              were Hausdorff, then the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_\alpha
              would be unique, and we would not need the axiom of choice at this step. The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+theorem"} ultrafilter theorem
              is used in step 3, and this is the only other place where a choice principle is needed. In other words, working over a choice-free set theory like
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZF
              or even BZ (bounded Zermelo set theory), the ultrafilter principle (UF) implies Tychonoff’s theorem for
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
          %h4#second_version Second version
          %p
            %a{:href => "#BourbakiGeneralTopology"} Bourbaki
            gives an alternative proof using ultrafilters, which may be formulated in terms of categorical lifting/extension properties. For any set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
