              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
              for that theory. A model exists iff a model exists for every theory generated by a finite subset of propositions, by the compactness theorem. In fact, the proof above suggests the topological provenance of the term “compactness theorem”.
          %h2#tychonoffs_theorem_for_locales Tychonoff’s theorem for locales
          %p
            Given the close connection between Tychonoff’s theorems and choice principles, it may come as a surprise that Tychonoff’s theorem for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            – the product of a small collection of compact locales is compact, equivalently that the coproduct of a small collection of compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            is compact – may be proved without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            and even
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructively
          %p
            More details to appear at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem+for+locales"} Tychonoff theorem for locales
          %h2#related_theorems Related theorems
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tietze+extension+theorem"} Tietze extension theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+invariance+of+dimension"} topological invariance of dimension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Brouwer's fixed point theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jordan+curve+theorem"} Jordan curve theorem
          %h2#References References
          %p The statement of Tychonoff’s theorem is made in
          %ul
            %li#Tychonoff35
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+N.+Tychonoff"}> A. N. Tychonoff
              , p. 272 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ein Fixpunktsatz
          %p
            where he says that the proof is the same as the one he gave for a product of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+intervals"} closed intervals
            in
          %ul
            %li#Tychonoff30
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+N.+Tychonoff"}> A. N. Tychonoff
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Über die topologische Erweiterung von Räumen
          %p An explicit proof was then given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduard+Cech"}> Eduard Cech
              , p. 830 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em On bicompact spaces
          %p The proof using ultrafilters is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"}> Henri Cartan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theorie des filtres
          %p and reproduced in
          %ul
            %li#BourbakiGeneralTopology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"}> Bourbaki
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em General Topology
          %p whence often known as “Bourbaki’s proof”.
          %ul
            %li#Cartan37
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"}> Henri Cartan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Filtres et ultrafiltres
          %p and its modern version is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kelley"}> John Kelley
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Convergence in topology
          %p The proof using convergence of nets is due to
          %ul
            %li#Chernoff92
